{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact head b68993ad6b4b8499a4bba3e81cb515f6f853b3dd under Hugh's request 3d936ff813dbadb689ed47b76d664db841270c1f. The shared session opener is sound within the existing trusted-local-resident boundary, but the implementation does not meet the request's explicit preflight condition.\n\nRequired correction: condition 1 names the preflight of gs state, batch, ratify and supersede, not only their reference resolution. This head wires newResolver to sessionSnapshot but leaves refuseIneffectiveAct -\u003e Workspace.PreflightAct and refuseIneffectiveBatch -\u003e Workspace.PreflightChain unchanged. internal/app/preflight.go:54 and :95 still call w.Snapshot, and a multi-act preview also builds a scratch fold from the local log. The report and architecture page correctly acknowledge that those commands still pay for local verification, but the report nevertheless says condition 1 is met. It is not. TestStateResolvesReferencesInTheResidentProjection proves citation resolution, while the no-local-loader test proves only loadSessionSnapshot, so neither demonstrates the requested preflight path.\n\nEither implement the preflight portion with the same validation and an end-to-end test showing the preflight's source and submitted act, while retaining the intended signing/admission boundaries, or obtain a durable revision from Hugh that explicitly accepts local fold preview and narrows condition 1 to composition/reference resolution. Correct the report's satisfaction claim and publish artifacts resting on that revision if the latter is chosen. This review is not asking to weaken admission or signing to save time; the current narrower result needs the requester's authority, as in the frontier review.\n\nArchitecture: the changed layer is 7, CLI orchestration; internal/eventref now documents resident-derived resolution and residentclient loses an unused warning helper. The new architecture artifact records acceptance of resident rows, the fixed response bound and profile limitation, the local preflight/admission exceptions and gs land's resident-derived ratification. All three documentation artifacts rest on the session_snapshot.go artifact. The source accurately implements that narrower contract, but it differs from the governing request at preflight.\n\nSecurity: the shared opener checks genesis, local sequence ref before and after the fetch, declared fold profile and decision-count/depth agreement. Failure reports the reason and uses the local audit. HTTP stays loopback-only with the existing no-credential/no-path URL rules and bounded response handling. These checks do not authenticate projection rows against the log: the resident can change default promise text, reference resolution and step-derived fields while keeping a correct head. The tests deliberately demonstrate that capability. It is acceptable only within the project's existing trusted local operator/resident boundary; the actor key is still read locally and the submitted acts retain normal admission. In particular gs land can sign a resident-derived ratification before the local merge-plan check, as the page states; the actual merge still validates the exact candidate and approval locally. Disclosure to stderr is visibility, not an approval gate. I found no further unresolved security defect under that stated trust model. Cached answers last for the command, so a kind introduced within a batch can produce a stale warning; it does not change admission.\n\nSimplification: one opener, one accepted snapshot per command and a shared resolver avoid duplicate full-status reads. Retaining the local preview can be a reasonable smaller scope, but only after the governing condition is revised. No additional cache protocol is needed for this review.\n\nValidation: independently ran the new session, fallback, step submission, work-next, reference resolution and vocabulary tests under -race at this head (13.229s, pass). Inspected lane3-all-r2.log: all 36 packages pass, exit 0; cmd/gs (638.006s) and docset logs also pass. The artifact records the required work-next before/after measurement (about 22s to 0.5–0.9s) and the unchanged local path. Checkout clean at b68993ad. This verdict names the reporting test artifact first and all twelve artifacts. Request a fresh review on a corrected implementation or duly revised basis.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:caa81f26836641f7b33883a5a20d5ce4b00313b2","binding":"assigned","head":"b68993ad6b4b8499a4bba3e81cb515f6f853b3dd","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c134b3608d809da0a639bf09bf771f967c138d1f\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:9cc06b6d5c931b5bbd1d20f6517a3d614ddaee09\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:9b74f3ab4a3eaa2b395055a7df7911ffd9db0384","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}